<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Hans Christian Ørsted</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1777">1777</a>–<a href="/years/1851">1851</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/19th-century">19th century</a> · Also known as Oersted</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Hans Christian Ørsted was a Danish chemist and physicist, born in Rudkøbing, who discovered that electric currents create magnetic fields. After a doctorate on the works of Kant in 1799 and three years of travel across <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/europe">Europe</a>, he became a professor at the University of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/copenhagen">Copenhagen</a> in 1806. In 1820 he published his finding that a nearby electric current deflected a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/magnetic-compass">compass</a> needle, for which the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/royal-society">Royal Society of London</a> awarded him the Copley Medal; the discovery led to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/andre-marie-ampere">André-Marie Ampère</a>'s work on electrodynamics and to the electric <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/telegraph">telegraph</a>. He also discovered aluminium, which he presented in 1825, and was a leader of the Danish Golden Age. He died in Copenhagen in 1851.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/hans-christian-rsted.jpg" alt="Hans Christian Ørsted" width="330" height="436" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Christian Albrecht Jensen · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:C.A._Jensen_-_Portrait_of_the_Physicist_Hans_Christian_Ørsted_-_KMS8176_-_Statens_Museum_for_Kunst.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
